# Ticket #004 – Switch Connection

**Status:** Resolved  
**Date:** 2026-07-31  
**Category:** Networking / Office / Switch / Ethernet  
**Environment:** Cisco  WRVS4400N Wireless-N Gigabit Security Router with VPN, PC running on Windows 11 Pro 

---

## Issue
The training room has very weak wifi signal from the main router in the server room in the floor below. Only a single ethernet wall port (female rj45 socket) connected to the main router is available for internet access in the entire training room.

## Observations  
- The router in this ticket does not have default access point operation mode.  

## Troubleshooting Steps
- Converted a decommissioned "Cisco WRVS4400N Wireless-N Gigabit Security Router with VPN" to serve as an access point (AP) through the following steps:
1. Power on the WRVS4400N router.
2. Directly connect the router to pc using rj45 ethernet cable (with one end of the cable in the pc & the other end into the router's WAN port).
2. Confirm successful connection & router details using the "ipconfig" command in the pc's cmd.
3. Record all router's details printed from the ipconfig results.
5. Copy the router's ethernet default gateway from the ipconfig result in order to access it's web interface for management settings.
6. Paste the copied default gateway ip address in the browser's address bar to access the router's management settings.
7. Logi into the router's web interface using valid credentials.
8. Navigate to Advanced Routing settings & change the "Operation Mode to "Router".
8. Navigate to the LAN settings.
9. In the LAN setting, change the router's local ip address from 192.168.1.1 to 192.168.1.3 (only adjust the host id, that is the last number in a valid ipv4 addresses; can change it to any unique & unused number in the subnet).  
10. Record the new router's local ip address for future management login access.  
11. Still inside the LAN settings, disable the dhcp server.
12. Click on save, then wait for the router to reboot. After reboot, router would serve as an access point (now converted to an AP, for this type of router without a default AP mode, the WAN port becomes useless).
13. Disconnect ethernet cable in step 1 from router's WAN port & reconnect into the router's LAN port 2 instead.
13. Access & log into the web interface again using 192.168.1.3 in the browser's address bar, in order to configure the SSID & security keys for wireless/wifi connections if required. Click save afterwards.
14. Connect router to the wall ethernet socket to connect it to the main router, by using another ethernet cable with one end of the cable into the wall socket, & the other end into the router's LAN port 1. 
20. Run ipconfig command in cmd to confirm a valid IPv4 address (192.168.1.x), & not a 169.254.0.x address.  
20. Verify internet connectivity.  

## Root Cause
Insufficient adjacent ethernet sockets (rj45 female sockets) to serve clients.

## Resolution
The issue was resolved by implementing a simple desktop switch to the network connection in order toexpand the LAN capacity;  
1. Connected the switch to the main router via one of the two available wall sockets using an ethernet cable with rj45 connectors.  
2. Connected the access point router to the main router via the second wall socket using an ethernet cable with rj45 connectors.  
3. Separately connected both user pc's to the switch in order to access the main router, using ethernet cables with rj45 connectors.  

## Verification
- Users successfully accessed the internet using wifi connection from the access point router.
- Users successfully accessed the main server through the main router via the LAN connections on the switch.  

## Status
Resolved. End-users confirmed operational.

## Notes
- For routers that do not have default access point (AP) modes (where you have to switch to AP mode by manually setting router's ip *[default gateway ip]* & disabling dchp), connect ethernet cable into the router's ethernet LAN 1 port & not the WAN port, for final connection to the main router or switch.
- For routers that have default access point (AP) modes, connect ethernet cable into the router's ethernet WAN port, for final connection to the main router or switch.


## Lessons Learned
- Familiarize with the type of router that is being used as access point, & take the notes above into consideration.

